---
import { C, FONT } from "../infographicTokens";

/**
 * Three different claims travel under the name "the 80% rule". Only one of them
 * is in the code. Separating them is the whole value of the article, because the
 * panel-capacity myth in particular sells unnecessary service upgrades.
 */
const claims = [
  {
    accent: C.green,
    verdict: "IN THE CODE",
    claim: "Continuous load ≤ 80% of the device",
    detail: "210.20(A) and 215.3 require the device to be sized at 125% of continuous load. Same rule, inverted.",
  },
  {
    accent: C.red,
    verdict: "NOT A CODE RULE",
    claim: "Never load a panel past 80% of its rating",
    detail: "No such requirement exists. Article 220 sizes the service; a correctly calculated 200 A service may carry 200 A.",
  },
  {
    accent: C.red,
    verdict: "NOT A CODE RULE",
    claim: "Never load any circuit past 80%",
    detail: "Non-continuous loads may use 100% of the rating. Most dwelling circuits have no continuous load at all.",
  },
];
---

<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 720 268" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
  <title>Three claims called the 80 percent rule, only one of them real</title>
  <desc>
    The requirement that continuous load not exceed 80 percent of the device rating is in the code, as the inverse of
    the 125 percent factor in 210.20(A) and 215.3. The claim that a panel must never be loaded past 80 percent of its
    rating is not a code rule — Article 220 sizes the service and a correctly calculated 200 amp service may carry
    200 amps. The claim that no circuit may be loaded past 80 percent is also wrong, because non-continuous loads may
    use the full rating.
  </desc>

  <rect x="8" y="8" width="704" height="252" rx="12" fill={C.panelBg} stroke={C.border} />
  <text x="24" y="36" fill={C.dark} font-family={FONT} font-size="14" font-weight="700">Three different claims share the name. One is real.</text>

  {claims.map((c, i) => {
    const y = 54 + i * 62;
    return (
      <g>
        <rect x="24" y={y} width="672" height="54" rx="9" fill={c.accent} opacity="0.07" />
        <rect x="24" y={y} width="672" height="54" rx="9" fill="none" stroke={c.accent} stroke-width="1.6" />
        <rect x="38" y={y + 11} width="112" height="18" rx="5" fill={c.accent} />
        <text x="94" y={y + 24} text-anchor="middle" fill={C.white} font-family={FONT} font-size="8" font-weight="800">{c.verdict}</text>
        <text x="164" y={y + 25} fill={C.dark} font-family={FONT} font-size="11.5" font-weight="800">{c.claim}</text>
        <text x="38" y={y + 45} fill={C.muted} font-family={FONT} font-size="9">{c.detail}</text>
      </g>
    );
  })}

  <text x="24" y="250" fill={C.muted} font-family={FONT} font-size="10">The panel myth is the expensive one — it talks people into service upgrades they don't need.</text>
</svg>
